@copilot Please generate a new issue (as a comment using markdown so I can copy/paste) titled "Remove remaining legacy v1 Driver and Application infrastructure".

Dive deep into this branch to determine the work involved in doing so. Read this for more context: https://github.com/gui-cs/Terminal.Gui/blob/31c8784c3421b1b4aa7fc2119359318e01da2dfe/MAINLOOP_DEEP_DIVE.md (that analyiss was done before this branch was completed so it might be slightly out of date).

Note, we do not yet want to do any renaming; that will be done in https://github.com/gui-cs/Terminal.Gui/pull/4337 after this PR (#4335) is merged and after the new Issue you are drafting is fixed.

> @copilot You missed the following APIs that are marked as `[Obsolete]`:
> 
> * `class LegacyTitledEnumSchema`
> * `class EnumSchema`
> 
> When removing these, there is code in `ElicitRequestParams` that needs to be removed:
> 
> * The `case "enumNames"` block is no longer needed, and the `enumNames` variable does not need to be defined or set
> * The block for `if (enumNames is not null)` will need to be removed as `psd = new EnumSchema` will no longer work. The `else` block will now always apply.
> * The `case LegacyTitledEnumSchema legacyEnum` block needs to be deleted
> * Unit tests specific to `EnumSchema` or `LegacyTitledEnumSchema` need to be deleted
> * Unit tests with `EnumSchema` or `LegacyTitledEnumSchema` scenarios mixed in need to have those scenarios removed from the tests; where applicable, scenarios for the following enum schemas should be used or added:
>   
>   * `TitledMultiSelectEnumSchema`
>   * `TitledSingleSelectEnumSchema`
>   * `UntitledMultiSelectEnumSchema`
>   * `UntitledSingleSelectEnumSchema`
> 
> Additionally, all places where `#pragma warning disable CS0618` is applied need to be removed, properly formatting the code around that removed block to ensure no double blank lines are left behind.
> 
> After these changes, there should not be any code search results for "[Obsolete" or "0618".

@jeffhandley, those just got merged as part of https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/pull/969, and I believe they'd still be needed for backwards wire compatibility.
```
/**
 * Use TitledSingleSelectEnumSchema instead.
 * This interface will be removed in a future version.
 */
export interface LegacyTitledEnumSchema {
  type: "string";
  title?: string;
  description?: string;
  enum: string[];
  /**
   * (Legacy) Display names for enum values.
   * Non-standard according to JSON schema 2020-12.
   */
  enumNames?: string[];
  default?: string;
}

// Union type for all enum schemas
export type EnumSchema =
  | SingleSelectEnumSchema
  | MultiSelectEnumSchema
  | LegacyTitledEnumSchema;
```

@steipete @mbelinky @gumadeiras 这条是 #14046 的底层修复（`agent.wait` 确定性返回 `finalAssistantText`），CI 已全绿，可与 #15383 二选一或互补合并。

---

@steipete @mbelinky @gumadeiras This is the infrastructure-layer fix for #14046 (`agent.wait` returns deterministic `finalAssistantText`). CI is fully green; it can be merged instead of #15383 or combined with it.

I now have a complete picture. Here is my review:

---

## PR #455 Review: `app: fix save command modal keyboard UX regression`

### Analysis

This PR fixes the same bug that PR #452 fixed in the Settings modal (`SettingsView.tsx`), but for `SaveCommandModal.tsx`. The backdrop `<div>` wrapping the dialog has an `onKeyDown` handler that was catching `Escape`, `Enter`, and `Space` — all three calling `onClose()`. Since keyboard events bubble from the inner `<input>` to the backdrop `<div>`, pressing Enter while typing a command name would bubble up and close the modal, making the form unusable via keyboard.

The 1-line fix (line 69) narrows the backdrop handler to only react to `Escape`, which is the correct behavior. This is consistent with the PR #452 fix applied to the Settings modal.

### Test Gap

The regression test has a structural problem. **Test 1** ("does not close the modal when Enter is pressed in the name input") fires `input.props.onKeyDown({ key: "Enter" })`, which directly invokes the input's `handleKeyDown` — a function that already never called `onClose` on Enter (it calls `handleSubmit`). TestRenderer doesn't simulate DOM event bubbling, so the backdrop's `onKeyDown` is never reached. This test passes **both before and after the fix**, which means it doesn't actually guard against the regression it claims to prevent.

A proper regression test needs to invoke the **backdrop**'s handler with Enter/Space and assert `onClose` is NOT called:

```tsx
it("does not close when Enter or Space is pressed on the backdrop", () => {
  const onClose = vi.fn();
  // ...create modal...
  const dialog = tree.root.findByProps({ role: "dialog" });
  act(() => {
    dialog.props.onKeyDown({ key: "Enter", preventDefault: vi.fn() });
    dialog.props.onKeyDown({ key: " ", preventDefault: vi.fn() });
  });
  expect(onClose).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

This test would fail on the old code and pass on the new code — that's what makes it a real regression test.

**Test 2** (Escape closes via backdrop) is correct and valuable.

### Structured Review

1. **Classification:** bug fix (keyboard UX regression)
2. **Scope verdict:** in scope — same pattern as merged PR #452
3. **Code quality:** pass — minimal, targeted 1-line fix
4. **Security:** clear — no attack surface changes
5. **Tests:** missing — test 1 does not actually detect the regression it guards against; needs a backdrop-level Enter/Space assertion as described above
6. **Decision:** REQUEST CHANGES

Add a test that fires `Enter` and `Space` on the **backdrop** (`dialog` element) and asserts `onClose` is not called. The current test 1 exercises the input's handler, which was never the source of the bug.

